   </div>
</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-16-bed-stuy/">← Back to District 16 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 16 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 16 (Bedford-Stuyvesant). Navigating the special education system can be challenging. This directory provides key contacts and crucial information to help you advocate for your child's needs.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 8 (Serving District 16)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>131 Livingston St, Brooklyn, NY 11201</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-935-4900">718-935-4900</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Persistence is key. Remember to document *everything* – dates, times, names of people you spoke with, and the content of your conversations. This documentation is invaluable should you need to file a formal complaint or request mediation.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window outlined in NYCRR 200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. Verbal requests hold little to no legal weight.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary reiterating your request and the date of the phone call.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Evaluation - [Student Name]"</em>, depending on the stage.</li>
                <li>Keep a detailed "Communication Log" of dates, times, names, titles, and a summary of the conversation. Include copies of all emails and letters sent and received.</li>
                <li>When sending a physical letter, send it via Certified Mail with Return Receipt Requested to confirm delivery.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, or if timelines are not being met, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call to the CSE.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and your child's teacher, if applicable). Request confirmation of receipt and a timeline for response.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent *and* District Superintendent. Explicitly state that you are escalating due to lack of response and potential violation of IDEA timelines (cite specific regulations if possible, e.g., "Failure to respond violates IDEA's requirement for timely evaluation as outlined in 34 CFR §300.301").</li>
                <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> Contact your City Council member and/or State Assembly member's office for assistance. Many have constituent services that can help navigate bureaucratic hurdles.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED (New York State Education Department) if services are denied or timelines are consistently violated.  Information on filing a complaint can be found on the NYSED website. This is a formal process, so ensure you have all your documentation in order. See 8 NYCRR §200.5(l) for complaint procedures.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="common-pitfalls" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District 16 Special Education Pitfalls
    </h2>

    <div class="pitfall-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Delayed Evaluations</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Districts sometimes struggle to meet the legally mandated 60-day timeline for completing initial evaluations (8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)).
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong>  Submit a written request for evaluation via certified mail to document the date.  If the timeline is approaching, send reminder emails and formally request an extension *in writing* if needed, but only if it benefits your child (e.g., waiting for a crucial medical report). If the district misses the deadline without a valid, documented reason, consider filing a State Complaint.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Inadequate IEP Goals</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                IEP goals that are not Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound (SMART) can hinder a child's progress (34 CFR §300.320).
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong>  Before the IEP meeting, review your child's present levels of performance and draft proposed IEP goals that are SMART.  Bring them to the meeting and be prepared to discuss and advocate for their inclusion.  Don't be afraid to ask for clarification on how progress will be measured.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Lack of Appropriate Services</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The IEP must include all necessary services to meet the child's unique needs (34 CFR §300.320).  This can include speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling, specialized academic instruction, and more.
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong> Research different service providers and programs in District 16. Come to the IEP meeting prepared to discuss specific programs or interventions that you believe would benefit your child. Obtain independent evaluations to support your requests. If the IEP doesn't include necessary services, formally disagree with the IEP and request mediation or file a due process complaint.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pitfall-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Placement Disputes</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Parents and the district may disagree on the most appropriate educational placement for the child (8 NYCRR §200.6).
            </p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong>  Visit potential placement options before the IEP meeting. Understand your child's right to be educated in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) – meaning they should be educated with typically developing peers to the maximum extent appropriate. If you disagree with the proposed placement, formally disagree with the IEP and request mediation or file a due process complaint to advocate for a more appropriate placement.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                The Committee on Special Education (CSE) handles special education services for students aged 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) handles services for children aged 3-5.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How do I request an evaluation for special education services in District 16?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                Submit a written request (via email or certified mail) to the CSE. The request should clearly state that you are requesting an evaluation to determine if your child is eligible for special education services. Include your child's name, date of birth, address, and a brief description of your concerns.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an IEP, and what should it include?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                An IEP (Individualized Education Program) is a legally binding document that outlines a child's special education needs, goals, and services. It should include: present levels of performance, measurable annual goals, a description of how progress will be measured, a statement of special education and related services, and a statement of any program modifications or supports. (34 CFR §300.320)
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are my rights as a parent in the special education process?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                You have numerous rights, including the right to: participate in all meetings regarding your child's special education, receive prior written notice of any proposed changes to your child's IEP, access your child's educational records, obtain an independent educational evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation, and file a complaint with NYSED or request mediation or a due process hearing if you disagree with the district's decisions. Refer to the "Parent's Guide to Special Education in New York State" for a comprehensive overview of your rights.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the IEP developed by the CSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                If you disagree with the IEP, you have several options: 1) You can formally disagree with the IEP in writing. 2) You can request a mediation with an impartial mediator to help resolve the dispute. 3) You can file a due process complaint to request a hearing before an impartial hearing officer (IHO). You also have the right to pursue resolution through a State Complaint.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
    </div>
</main>
